1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,880
A few people ask me what you're actually meant to do as Scout when your team's on defense, or getting completely rolled,

2
00:00:04,880 --> 00:00:08,160
or you're playing on some narrow map like Dust Bowl, where there's barely anywhere to move.

3
00:00:08,160 --> 00:00:12,060
And they all sound like different questions, but it's pretty much the same problem.

4
00:00:12,060 --> 00:00:16,280
You can't just hold W and take whatever fight you want anymore, because Scout feels

5
00:00:16,799 --> 00:00:21,000
ridiculous when your team's doing well. Everyone's distracted, people are getting separated,

6
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:25,080
you've got space to run around, and you're usually the first person there whenever a fight opens up.

7
00:00:25,200 --> 00:00:29,299
But the second your team loses all that pressure and every doorway starts getting watched,

8
00:00:29,639 --> 00:00:35,000
Scout can go from feeling like he has no limitations, to feeling like one of the most useless classes in the game.

9
00:00:38,560 --> 00:00:42,560
So a lot of this video is going to be about slowing down a bit, using chip damage,

10
00:00:42,680 --> 00:00:47,480
sitting further back on high ground where you can actually see what's going on, and waiting for someone to overextend

11
00:00:47,480 --> 00:00:50,720
instead of launching yourself into three people every time you see their backs turned.

12
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:53,400
Because even when an opening looks really good,

13
00:00:53,400 --> 00:00:59,080
you still have to think about whether your team is alive, whether anyone can actually help you, and whether dying for that play is even

14
00:00:59,080 --> 00:01:05,500
worth it. Especially when you're defending on red in payload or attack-defend, because you can't take your life for granted like Blue Team Cat,

15
00:01:05,500 --> 00:01:09,919
because those respawn times on certain maps get just ridiculous, and

16
00:01:10,360 --> 00:01:13,680
one bad death can take you out of the fight for what feels like forever.

17
00:01:14,279 --> 00:01:18,080
And Scout can create way more for his team than the scoreboard actually shows.

18
00:01:18,199 --> 00:01:23,519
You can make half the enemy team turn around, pull someone away from the objective, or force a medic backwards,

19
00:01:23,519 --> 00:01:27,320
but your team still has to notice and do something with it.

20
00:01:27,320 --> 00:01:33,120
So we're going to go through all of that with some pre-recorded clips that I'm going to lightly commentate, and how to stay useful when

21
00:01:33,120 --> 00:01:37,760
you can't hold W. How to know when to commit, and how to tell when you made a bad play,

22
00:01:38,040 --> 00:01:41,800
versus when your team just didn't take advantage of what you created.

23
00:01:42,480 --> 00:01:46,440
In downtime situations like this on defense, chip damage is sometimes all you can really do,

24
00:01:46,440 --> 00:01:52,639
but it's more important than people think. It makes people look back, slows them down, and forces them to look for heals. And make sure

25
00:01:52,639 --> 00:01:58,080
not just when pestering people, but in general, make sure you're on an elevated platform when holding any position with Scout.

26
00:01:58,160 --> 00:02:03,599
It's crucial that if you get the chance, to not let engineers get their sentry built in the first place. If it's level 3,

27
00:02:03,599 --> 00:02:08,240
you alert your team, and when your combo comes out to deal with it, help them if you can at that very moment,

28
00:02:08,240 --> 00:02:13,360
but otherwise pay attention to the flanks. The engineer was on the ball here, and he starts building again,

29
00:02:13,360 --> 00:02:16,360
so I call it out to my soldier friend, and he deals with it straight away.

30
00:02:17,880 --> 00:02:24,119
The engineer's probably thinking, oh my god, I'm so unlucky, they keep walking out as soon as I build my stuff, but it isn't luck,

31
00:02:24,119 --> 00:02:30,320
I'm scouting out these areas, I'm being an actual Scout, and I'm communicating what I see to my team. At the same time,

32
00:02:30,320 --> 00:02:33,399
I'm not overextending, I'm treating these two flanks like they're my post,

33
00:02:33,399 --> 00:02:39,160
I'm contesting them, and I'm cleaning up anyone who tries to walk through, but I'm not running forward trying to take a 1v2,

34
00:02:39,279 --> 00:02:45,479
I'm just taking it slow, taking my time, and making sure these areas stay contested, because after all, we're playing defense,

35
00:02:45,479 --> 00:02:50,199
we're not the ones attacking, you don't need to hold W as much as you might think, just because you're playing Scout.

36
00:02:50,199 --> 00:02:52,199
Nah, nah, nah, nah, nah.

37
00:03:04,080 --> 00:03:07,080
Right here, I'm backed into a corner by a Pyro and a Scout.

38
00:03:07,080 --> 00:03:11,080
It doesn't look like I have anywhere to go, but there's this tiny slope in the ground.

39
00:03:11,080 --> 00:03:16,600
I run up it, jump backwards, and it gives me just enough height to double jump forward over the Pyro's head.

40
00:03:16,600 --> 00:03:21,559
That tiny bit of elevation saves me from getting flamed, and just happens to set me up to kill the Scout as well.

41
00:03:21,639 --> 00:03:26,199
That's one of the best skills you can have as Scout, not just seamlessly using anything around you,

42
00:03:26,199 --> 00:03:29,399
but identifying what can be used as high ground in the first place.

43
00:03:29,479 --> 00:03:34,520
It's a bit like Emmett becoming a master builder at the end of the Lego movie, except you're looking at every box,

44
00:03:35,039 --> 00:03:38,520
railing, or tiny slope and thinking, can I use that as high ground?

45
00:03:38,919 --> 00:03:43,600
Basically, any type of elevator platform will let you get extra height and reposition with less risk.

46
00:03:43,800 --> 00:03:49,600
Like the last clip, this is a defensive situation that isn't as straightforward as holding a boxed-in last with two obvious flanks.

47
00:03:49,600 --> 00:03:53,000
When you're forced to fight in more open areas without much pressure from your team,

48
00:03:53,000 --> 00:03:57,600
Scout needs to take some of that pressure off them by becoming the center of attention without just throwing his life away.

49
00:03:57,600 --> 00:04:01,600
It's about occupying spaces the enemy can't ignore, forcing them to deal with you,

50
00:04:01,600 --> 00:04:05,600
and drawing attention away from your team while still prioritizing survivability.

51
00:04:14,600 --> 00:04:20,600
Once your team is no longer occupying the space you were trying to defend, and the enemy has taken full control of that area,

52
00:04:20,600 --> 00:04:22,600
your presence there becomes much less valuable.

53
00:04:22,600 --> 00:04:25,600
If you still have space behind you to retreat into, use it.

54
00:04:25,600 --> 00:04:30,600
There's no reason to keep testing an area your team no longer has any presence in, especially when you're on defense.

55
00:04:30,600 --> 00:04:37,600
Your job isn't to take the space back by yourself, it's to stay alive, continue slowing them down, and keep running time off the clock.

56
00:04:37,600 --> 00:04:39,600
But you need to be alive to do this.

57
00:04:39,600 --> 00:04:45,600
Where Blue Team is all about trying to get one big coordinated push to break through, Red is more about survivability.

58
00:04:51,600 --> 00:04:56,600
The previous clip was a good example of what Scout should be doing when his team doesn't have much presence in the area,

59
00:04:56,600 --> 00:05:01,600
but when your team is established and actually occupying the space you're defending, your role changes.

60
00:05:01,600 --> 00:05:07,600
Now your job is to prevent threats from reaching your team by pestering them or killing them like this sniper I focus in on,

61
00:05:07,600 --> 00:05:10,600
to prevent him landing an easy headshot on my heavy or med,

62
00:05:10,600 --> 00:05:14,600
and clean up easy picks on enemies your power class is already engaging with,

63
00:05:14,600 --> 00:05:18,600
and safely apply chip damage that forces Blue to retreat or delays their push.

64
00:05:18,600 --> 00:05:24,600
You're still trying to have as much impact as possible, but without overcommitting and throwing away the position your team has already established.

65
00:05:37,600 --> 00:05:51,600
Another question I got was how you're meant to play Scout on narrow maps like Dustbowl.

66
00:05:51,600 --> 00:05:56,600
Honestly, maps like Dustbowl and Gold Rush are where you need to take all of this advice the most seriously.

67
00:05:56,600 --> 00:06:01,600
They're old, restrictive maps where the balances age pretty badly, especially for Red.

68
00:06:01,600 --> 00:06:06,600
Your respawn can be anywhere from 18 to 22 seconds, and the points can be captured absurdly quickly.

69
00:06:06,600 --> 00:06:11,600
And because of that, there isn't some secret flank or defensive strategy that suddenly makes Scout amazing.

70
00:06:11,600 --> 00:06:17,600
You have to be extremely deliberate about where you position yourself, which fights you take, and whether you're actually where your team needs you.

71
00:06:17,600 --> 00:06:21,600
High ground is still important, but only if it's actually safe.

72
00:06:21,600 --> 00:06:26,600
Most of the obvious high ground on the first section of Dustbowl leaves you exposed to snipers and spam.

73
00:06:26,600 --> 00:06:32,600
Sometimes you genuinely have to play around the point, and be prepared to back up once your team loses that space.

74
00:06:33,600 --> 00:06:39,600
In this clip, I move around and help my team defend the choke, but I always end up returning to the same flank besides the point.

75
00:06:39,600 --> 00:06:44,600
It gives me cover, close range fights, and an easy escape, while still allowing me to directly defend the point.

76
00:06:44,600 --> 00:06:50,600
It's also a really common place for Blue to put a teleporter, so it's naturally somewhere Scout can find a lot of value.

77
00:06:50,600 --> 00:06:52,600
Target priority is also important.

78
00:06:52,600 --> 00:06:58,600
In general, if you see something like a direct hit soldier, there's a good chance he's using it because he wants to destroy your team's sentry nest.

79
00:06:58,600 --> 00:07:04,600
Scout can choose his fights, so you should use that freedom to focus the people who are most threatening to your team.

80
00:07:04,600 --> 00:07:08,600
You need to play as a team better than Blue does on old maps like Gold Rush and Dustbowl.

81
00:07:08,600 --> 00:07:14,600
Something I like doing is briefly moving into Blue's territory, and getting someone's attention, and then immediately retreating.

82
00:07:14,600 --> 00:07:19,600
Impatient players will often overreact and chase you too far, which brings them directly into your team.

83
00:07:19,600 --> 00:07:28,600
That's what happens to this Pyro when he follows me up the stairs just then, and my soldier friend already was holding that position behind me, and I knew that, so he deals with the Pyro straight away.

84
00:07:28,600 --> 00:07:33,600
I'm not just running away from the fight, I'm dragging the Pyro into a fight that's much better for my team.

85
00:07:33,600 --> 00:07:42,600
Give Callouts a Scout, because I've already mentioned this, but he's called Scout for a reason, and you're often the first person who can see what Blue's doing,

86
00:07:42,600 --> 00:07:45,600
so make your team aware of anything they deserve to know about.

87
00:07:45,600 --> 00:07:50,600
It doesn't need to be complicated, you know, it's just that their combo is coming through the top left flank,

88
00:07:50,600 --> 00:07:54,600
or a Pyro is jetpacking towards you, or someone is moving into your team's space.

89
00:07:54,600 --> 00:07:58,600
Even basic information just gives your team time to react and capitalise.

90
00:08:00,600 --> 00:08:04,600
Now I know not everyone wants to treat Casual like a scrim and constantly give out Callouts,

91
00:08:04,600 --> 00:08:08,600
but this Sixtus clip is the clearest way I can show you how powerful they are,

92
00:08:08,600 --> 00:08:13,600
and how powerful Scout becomes when his team can actually capitalise on the openings he creates.

93
00:08:13,600 --> 00:08:20,600
I don't create this opening by dropping the Medic, I create it by forcing basically their entire team to turn around and focus on me.

94
00:08:20,600 --> 00:08:22,600
For those few seconds, none of them are looking at my team,

95
00:08:22,600 --> 00:08:27,600
which gives everyone else the opportunity to move forward and wipe out basically their entire team.

96
00:08:27,600 --> 00:08:29,600
But that's only powerful if my team knows what's happening,

97
00:08:29,600 --> 00:08:35,599
I can't expect them to capitalise on the entire enemy team looking at me unless I call it out and make them aware.

98
00:08:35,599 --> 00:08:40,599
Once I do, they know that their opening exists, and can immediately take advantage of it.

99
00:08:40,599 --> 00:08:44,599
And this is what it looks like in full swing when your team capitalises on the opening Scout creates.

100
00:08:44,599 --> 00:08:49,599
Your value isn't always the player you kill, sometimes it's how much attention you pull away from everyone else,

101
00:08:49,599 --> 00:08:52,599
and this absolutely applies to Casual as well.

102
00:08:52,599 --> 00:08:56,599
People just don't communicate it nearly enough, especially a Scout.

103
